{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3516","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3516","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3516\",\"parent_revision\":\"7e58713acf0d1d93c0988760782557fa1ff4bc1222c35dc841dd65253729238b\",\"root_revision\":\"7e58713acf0d1d93c0988760782557fa1ff4bc1222c35dc841dd65253729238b\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-09-13\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nresearch-curator's PR #3513 wires check-backlinks --fix into the shared Post-Actions step as a trusted per-run gate for all four modes. Two gaps surfaced by Codex review during that PR make its exit code and coverage less trustworthy than the new caller assumes:\\n\\n1. build_cross_reference_graph() in backlink_lib.py catches OSError (unreadable file) and ValueError (malformed Cross-References table) per-file with no warning emitted -- its own docstring states paths that cannot be resolved are \\\"silently skipped.\\\" check-backlinks calls this same function for both its initial scan and its post-repair verification scan, and reports exit 0 whenever no asymmetric edges remain in the graph it built. If the scan silently dropped some files due to a read or parse error, exit 0 means \\\"no known-bad edges in what was successfully scanned,\\\" not \\\"the whole vault was verified\\\" -- a false completeness signal.\\n\\n2. check-backlinks --fix has no per-file exclude/skip mechanism -- it operates on the whole vault directory with no way to keep it from touching a specific target file. A caller that wants to avoid writing into a file with pre-existing uncommitted changes from another contributor can only detect and exclude that file from its own commit after check-backlinks has already run and potentially written to it, not prevent the write in the first place.\\n\\n**User-provided context**: possible fixes suggested during the PR #3513 review discussion -- (1) make build_cross_reference_graph/check_backlinks surface or fail on scan-phase read/parse errors instead of swallowing them silently, so a clean exit code is a trustworthy completeness signal; (2) add an --exclude/--skip-path option (or equivalent) to check-backlinks so a caller can keep it from writing to specific files at all.\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Agent task - auto-derived from input\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-09-13\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Follow Up\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-13T02:41:39.819657Z</sub>\\n\\nRound 2 finding from the same PR #3513 review: _repair_one_asymmetric_pair()'s entry-name bug (fixed in PR #3513 commit 1938aaf88) was corrected to use source.stem instead of the target's name, but source.stem is still just the filename slug, not the entry's actual display title -- e.g. research/agent-frameworks/Trellis.md declares `title: Trellis \u2014 Multi-Platform AI Coding Framework` in its YAML frontmatter but the repaired backlink row shows the raw slug \\\"Trellis\\\"; claude-code-harness.md shows lowercase-hyphenated \\\"claude-code-harness\\\" instead of a proper display name. This also affects this function's pre-existing (before PR #3513) fallback branch, which has used source.stem all along.\\n\\nNot fixed in PR #3513: the vault has at least two historical frontmatter schemas for an entry's own display-name field (`name:` in one schema, `title:` in another, per commit 01a64e5db's schema-reconciliation work), and this codebase has no existing alias-resolution helper for that specific field (only for the four REQUIRED_HEADER_FIELDS). Resolving it properly requires deciding a canonical resolution order across the historical schema variants -- a bigger, separately-scoped change than the wrong-entity bug this item was originally filed for.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"e07c70f081423491ccfee41d2d9a7fb48a12dc348235927ce25d990118a493f0\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABUMko_g\"}"}